window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-bio-inheritance","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation · Practice","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Morgan coined a term for the generation of non-parental gene combinations in a dihybrid cross. That term is:","image":"","options":["Linkage","Segregation","Recombination","Independent assortment"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Morgan used 'recombination' for the appearance of non-parental gene combinations. He used 'linkage' for the physical association of genes on the same chromosome, so linkage is the opposite idea and a common trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Dominance is not an autonomous feature of a gene.\nReason (R): In pea, the same allele pair (B, b) shows complete dominance when seed shape is taken as the phenotype, but incomplete dominance when starch grain size is taken as the phenotype.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Bb seeds are round like BB seeds, but their starch grains are of intermediate size. Whether an allele appears dominant therefore depends on the phenotype examined, which is why dominance is not autonomous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Multiple alleles of a gene, such as the three alleles of gene I, can be identified only through population studies because","image":"","options":["Multiple alleles arise only in large populations through mutation","A diploid individual can carry at most two alleles of a gene","Each allele is expressed only in a different sex","Multiple alleles are always recessive and hidden in individuals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A diploid individual has only two copies of a gene, so it can show at most two of the three alleles. The full set of alleles becomes evident only when many individuals in a population are examined.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which genetic disorder is described as a quantitative problem, in which too few globin molecules are synthesised, rather than a qualitative defect in the globin itself?","image":"","options":["Sickle-cell anaemia","Thalassemia","Haemophilia","Phenylketonuria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In thalassemia, one of the globin chains (α or β) is synthesised at a reduced rate, so the defect lies in quantity. Sickle-cell anaemia is the qualitative counterpart: an incorrectly functioning globin results from the Glu→Val substitution.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which scientist combined the knowledge of chromosomal segregation with Mendelian principles and named this synthesis the chromosomal theory of inheritance?","image":"","options":["Thomas Hunt Morgan","Walter Sutton","Alfred Sturtevant","Hugo de Vries"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Sutton and Boveri noticed that chromosomes and genes behave in parallel. It was Sutton who united chromosomal segregation with Mendel's principles and called it the chromosomal theory of inheritance. Morgan later verified the theory experimentally; he did not propose it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (disorder) with List-II (genetic basis):\nList-I\nA. Down's syndrome\nB. Turner's syndrome\nC. Klinefelter's syndrome\nD. Sickle-cell anaemia\nList-II\n(i) 47, XXY karyotype\n(ii) Single base substitution GAG to GUG in the sixth codon of the $\\beta$-globin gene\n(iii) Trisomy of chromosome 21\n(iv) 45, X0 karyotype","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Down's syndrome is trisomy 21, Turner's syndrome is 45, X0 and Klinefelter's syndrome is 47, XXY. Sickle-cell anaemia is a point mutation (GAG to GUG). The common trap is swapping the X0 and XXY karyotypes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In the F2 generation of Mendel's dihybrid cross RRYY × rryy, the Punnett square has 16 boxes. How many different genotypes are represented in these 16 boxes?","image":"","options":["4","16","6","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Each gene gives 3 genotypes (RR, Rr, rr and YY, Yy, yy), so there are $3 \\times 3 = 9$ distinct genotypes. Several boxes repeat the same genotype. The number 4 is the count of phenotypes, and 16 is only the number of boxes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Alfred Sturtevant used the frequency of recombination between gene pairs on the same chromosome as a measure of the distance between them, and mapped their positions.\nStatement II: Genetic maps have been used as a starting point for sequencing whole genomes, as in the Human Genome Sequencing Project.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Sturtevant, Morgan's student, treated recombination frequency as a measure of the distance between genes when he built chromosome maps. Such genetic maps are now a starting point for whole-genome sequencing, so both statements are correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In 1891, Henking traced a specific nuclear structure through spermatogenesis in a few insects and named it the 'X body'. Which of the following correctly describes what he observed?","image":"","options":["Every sperm received the X body after spermatogenesis","About 50 per cent of the sperm received the X body and the other 50 per cent did not","The X body was present only in eggs and never in sperm","The X body disappeared completely before sperm formation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Henking found that only half of the sperm received the X body. He could not explain its significance. Later workers showed that it was a chromosome, which they named the X-chromosome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In phenylketonuria, phenylalanine accumulates in the body and is converted into which compound, whose build-up in the brain leads to mental retardation?","image":"","options":["Tyrosine","Homogentisic acid","Uric acid","Phenylpyruvic acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Affected persons lack the enzyme that converts phenylalanine into tyrosine. The excess phenylalanine is converted into phenylpyruvic acid and its derivatives, which harm the brain and are also excreted in urine. Tyrosine is the product that is not formed, so it is the trap option.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A violet-flowered pea plant (violet V dominant over white v) was test crossed and produced 62 violet-flowered and 58 white-flowered plants. If this same violet plant were self-pollinated instead, what fraction of the violet-flowered progeny would be expected to be heterozygous?","image":"","options":["1/4","1/2","2/3","3/4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The roughly 1:1 test-cross result shows the parent is Vv. Selfing Vv gives 1 VV : 2 Vv : 1 vv, so among violet progeny, 2 out of 3 are Vv. Answering 1/2 gives the fraction among all progeny, not among violet ones.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Both the XO and the XY mechanisms of sex determination are examples of male heterogamety.\nStatement II: In birds, the total number of chromosomes in the female is different from that in the male.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In both the XO and XY types, males produce two kinds of gametes: with or without X, or X and Y. Both are therefore male heterogamety. In birds, however, males and females have the same total chromosome number; only the sex chromosomes differ (ZZ versus ZW).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (disorder) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\n(A) Sickle-cell anaemia\n(B) Thalassemia\n(C) Phenylketonuria\n(D) Haemophilia\nList-II\n(I) Too few globin molecules are synthesised (quantitative defect)\n(II) Queen Victoria was a carrier\n(III) An incorrectly functioning globin is synthesised (qualitative defect)\n(IV) Derivatives of an amino acid are excreted in urine because the kidney absorbs them poorly\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sickle-cell anaemia is a qualitative globin defect, while thalassemia is a quantitative one. In PKU, phenylpyruvic acid and related compounds appear in urine, and Queen Victoria's pedigree is the classic haemophilia example. Swapping the qualitative and quantitative labels is the usual mistake.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about a male (drone) honey bee is correct?","image":"","options":["It develops from a fertilised egg and has 32 chromosomes","It produces sperms by mitosis","It has a father but no grandfather","It can have sons but cannot have grandsons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A drone develops from an unfertilised egg by parthenogenesis and is haploid with 16 chromosomes, so it forms sperms by mitosis. It has no father and cannot have sons, but it has a grandfather and can have grandsons.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In Morgan's Drosophila crosses, the genes white (w) and yellow (y) showed 1.3% recombination, while white (w) and miniature wing (m) showed 37.2% recombination. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In both crosses, parental gene combinations outnumbered non-parental combinations.\n(ii) Genes y and w are more tightly linked than genes w and m.\n(iii) The F2 of these dihybrid crosses gave the 9:3:3:1 ratio expected for independent genes.\n(iv) These genes are located on the Y chromosome.\n(v) Recombination refers to the generation of non-parental gene combinations.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (v) are correct, since 37.2% recombinants still means 62.8% parental types. Statement (iii) is wrong because the F2 ratio deviated markedly from 9:3:3:1. Statement (iv) is wrong because these genes lie on the X chromosome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A haemophilic man marries a woman who is a carrier for haemophilia. Which of the following correctly describes the expected children?","image":"","options":["All daughters are carriers and none of the sons is haemophilic","Half the daughters are haemophilic and the rest are carriers; half the sons are haemophilic","Half the daughters are carriers and the rest are normal; half the sons are haemophilic","All sons are haemophilic and all daughters are normal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Every daughter gets $X^h$ from her father and either $X^h$ or $X$ from her mother, so half are haemophilic and half are carriers. Sons get their X only from the mother, so half are affected. This mating is exactly the rare situation needed to produce a haemophilic female.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A person with genotype $I^Ai$ has blood group A.\nReason (R): Allele $i$ does not produce any sugar, so only the sugar coded by $I^A$ appears on the RBC surface.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In an $I^Ai$ person only $I^A$ contributes a sugar, because $i$ produces none. The phenotype is therefore group A. This lack of a product from $i$ is exactly why $I^A$ is dominant over it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A change in a single base pair of DNA is called a point mutation. Which of the following disorders is the classical example of such a mutation?","image":"","options":["Down's syndrome","Sickle-cell anaemia","Turner's syndrome","Klinefelter's syndrome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Sickle-cell anaemia results from a single base change in the beta-globin gene, which makes it a point mutation. The other three are chromosomal disorders caused by a gain or loss of whole chromosomes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A person with phenylketonuria lacks the enzyme that converts phenylalanine into another amino acid. Which compound builds up as a derivative of the accumulated phenylalanine?","image":"","options":["Tyrosine","Homogentisic acid","Uric acid","Phenylpyruvic acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Without the enzyme, phenylalanine is not converted to tyrosine. It accumulates and is turned into phenylpyruvic acid and related derivatives, which damage the brain and are excreted in urine. Tyrosine is the product the patient fails to make, so it is not the compound that builds up.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A true-breeding round, yellow pea plant (RRYY) is crossed with a wrinkled, green plant (rryy). The F1 is self-pollinated. Match List-I (feature of F2) with List-II (value) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\nA. Number of different genotypes\nB. Number of different phenotypes\nC. Fraction of plants with genotype RrYy\nD. Fraction of plants with genotype rrYy\nList-II\n(i) 4\n(ii) $\\frac{1}{8}$\n(iii) 9\n(iv) $\\frac{1}{4}$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Independent assortment gives $3 \\times 3 = 9$ genotypes and $2 \\times 2 = 4$ phenotypes. RrYy has a probability of $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$, and rrYy has $\\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{8}$. A common trap is mixing up the counts of genotypes and phenotypes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Pink snapdragons (Rr) are self-pollinated to obtain an F2 generation. All white-flowered F2 plants are discarded, and every remaining F2 plant is self-pollinated. Assuming each plant leaves an equal number of offspring, what fraction of the resulting F3 progeny will be white-flowered?","image":"","options":["1/4","1/3","1/6","1/12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Among the retained F2 plants, 1/3 are red (RR) and 2/3 are pink (Rr). Only the pink plants produce white offspring, and 1/4 of their progeny are white, so the fraction is 2/3 × 1/4 = 1/6. The key trap is forgetting to remove the discarded white plants before calculating proportions.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (character of pea studied by Mendel) with List-II (contrasting traits):\nList-I\nA. Flower position\nB. Pod shape\nC. Stem height\nD. Flower colour\nList-II\nI. Violet/white\nII. Tall/dwarf\nIII. Axial/terminal\nIV. Inflated/constricted\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The correct pairs are flower position with axial/terminal, pod shape with inflated/constricted, stem height with tall/dwarf, and flower colour with violet/white. A common mix-up is to swap the two positional and shape terms.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A drone honey bee mates with a queen, and the queen uses only his sperm. Which of the following correctly describes the progeny?","image":"","options":["Fertilised eggs give drones and workers in a 1:1 ratio","The drone's sperm carry 8 chromosomes each, so the fertilised eggs have 24 chromosomes","Some of the drone's sons will inherit his chromosomes","All daughters receive an identical paternal set of 16 chromosomes, because the drone forms sperm by mitosis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A haploid drone (16 chromosomes) makes sperm by mitosis, so every sperm carries the same 16 chromosomes. All daughters (females with 32 chromosomes) therefore get an identical paternal set. Fertilised eggs never become drones, so a drone cannot have sons.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A tall plant is picked at random from the F2 generation of a cross between true-breeding tall (TT) and dwarf (tt) pea plants. It is then self-pollinated. What is the probability that a seed obtained from this selfing grows into a dwarf plant?","image":"","options":["1/4","1/6","1/3","1/8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A random tall F2 plant is Tt with probability 2/3, and only a Tt plant can produce dwarf offspring, which it does 1/4 of the time on selfing. So the probability is 2/3 × 1/4 = 1/6. Answering 1/4 wrongly assumes the tall plant is certainly Tt.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In the F2 of a dihybrid cross RRYY × rryy, what fraction of the plants will breed true for both seed shape and seed colour when self-pollinated?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{16}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{9}{16}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only plants homozygous at both loci breed true: RRYY, RRyy, rrYY and rryy. Each of these occurs at $\\frac{1}{16}$, so together they make $\\frac{4}{16} = \\frac{1}{4}$. The common trap is to count only the parental type RRYY, which gives $\\frac{1}{16}$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In sickle-cell anaemia, the sixth codon of the beta-globin gene changes from GAG to GUG, so glutamic acid is replaced by valine.\nStatement II: This change is a frame-shift mutation caused by the insertion of a base pair.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The sickle-cell defect is a single base substitution (A to U in the codon), which is a point mutation. It is not an insertion, so Statement II is wrong. A substitution changes only one codon and does not shift the reading frame.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"All the tall plants of the F2 generation of a monohybrid cross (TT × tt) are self-pollinated, and their progeny are pooled. Assuming each plant leaves an equal number of offspring, what fraction of the F3 progeny is expected to be dwarf?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{1}{6}$","$\\frac{1}{3}$","$\\frac{1}{8}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Among the tall F2 plants, $\\frac{1}{3}$ are TT and $\\frac{2}{3}$ are Tt. Only the Tt plants produce dwarfs on selfing, at a rate of $\\frac{1}{4}$. So the fraction of dwarfs is $\\frac{2}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{6}$. The trap is answering $\\frac{1}{4}$ by assuming every tall plant is Tt.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In a human pedigree chart, a male (square) and a female (circle) are joined by a double horizontal line. This symbol represents:","image":"","options":["Mating between unaffected individuals","Mating between relatives (consanguineous mating)","Parents of identical twins","A divorced couple"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A double line between a male and a female denotes consanguineous mating, which means mating between relatives. Ordinary mating is shown by a single horizontal line.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"When pink-flowered F1 snapdragon plants (Rr) are self-pollinated, what is the phenotypic ratio of red : pink : white in the F2 generation?","image":"","options":["3 : 0 : 1","1 : 2 : 1","9 : 6 : 1","1 : 1 : 2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Selfing Rr gives RR : Rr : rr in the ratio 1:2:1. Because each genotype has a distinct colour (red, pink, white), the phenotypic ratio is also 1:2:1, not the usual 3:1.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"During oogenesis in a woman, the X chromatids fail to segregate, so an egg carrying two X chromosomes is formed. If this egg is fertilised by a normal Y-bearing sperm, the resulting individual would most likely have:","image":"","options":["Turner's syndrome with karyotype 45, XO","Down's syndrome with karyotype 47, +21","A normal male karyotype 46, XY","Klinefelter's syndrome with karyotype 47, XXY"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"An XX egg combined with a Y sperm gives XXY, which with 44 autosomes is 47, XXY, or Klinefelter's syndrome. This is aneuploidy caused by failure of chromatid segregation. Turner's syndrome would need a gamete lacking an X, and Down's syndrome involves chromosome 21.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which of the following is a classic example of a polygenic trait in humans?","image":"","options":["ABO blood group","Human skin colour","Red-green colour blindness","Sickle-cell anaemia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Human skin colour, like human height, varies along a continuous gradient and is controlled by several genes with additive effects. The other options are each governed by a single gene.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The data collected by Mendel in his pea experiments carried greater credibility.\nReason (R): Mendel's experiments had a large sampling size, and he analysed the results statistically.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the large sampling size gave Mendel's data greater credibility. Applying statistical analysis to biology for the first time further strengthened his conclusions, so R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assume human skin colour is controlled by three genes A, B and C, with dominant alleles adding to darkness. Consider the following statements:\n(i) The effect of each dominant allele is additive.\n(ii) The environment can also influence the phenotype.\n(iii) Individuals with genotypes AaBbCc and AABbcc are expected to have the same skin colour.\n(iv) Polygenic traits show distinct alternate forms, like Mendel's pea characters.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iii) are correct; both genotypes in (iii) carry three dominant alleles, so additive effects give the same intermediate shade. Statement (iv) is wrong because polygenic traits vary along a continuous gradient.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"NCERT uses myotonic dystrophy in a representative pedigree to illustrate which pattern of inheritance?","image":"","options":["Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","Autosomal dominant","Y-linked"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Myotonic dystrophy is the example given for an autosomal dominant trait. Sickle-cell anaemia is the example given for an autosomal recessive trait.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A man with blood group A and a woman with blood group B have a first child with blood group O. What is the probability that their second child will have blood group AB?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{3}{4}$","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An O child ($ii$) must receive $i$ from each parent, so the parents are $I^Ai$ and $I^Bi$. In the cross $I^Ai \\times I^Bi$, the four outcomes $I^AI^B$, $I^Ai$, $I^Bi$ and $ii$ are equally likely, so the chance of AB is $\\frac{1}{4}$. Each birth is independent, so the first child's group does not change this probability.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"The human ABO blood group is controlled by gene I, which has three alleles: $I^A$, $I^B$ and $i$. How many different genotypes and how many different phenotypes (blood groups) are possible in the human population?","image":"","options":["6 genotypes and 4 phenotypes","9 genotypes and 4 phenotypes","6 genotypes and 6 phenotypes","3 genotypes and 3 phenotypes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Three alleles give six two-allele combinations: $I^AI^A$, $I^Ai$, $I^BI^B$, $I^Bi$, $I^AI^B$ and $ii$. These produce only four blood groups (A, B, AB and O) because $I^A$ and $I^B$ are dominant over $i$. Counting 9 treats ordered parental combinations as different genotypes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the Punnett square, as used to analyse Mendel's monohybrid cross?","image":"","options":["A graphical method, developed by the British geneticist Reginald C. Punnett, for calculating the probability of all possible offspring genotypes in a genetic cross","A chart developed by Gregor Mendel to record the phenotypes of seven pairs of contrasting traits in pea","A diagram developed by Walter Sutton showing how chromosomes move during meiosis","A family tree used to trace the inheritance of a trait over several human generations"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R.C. Punnett devised the square. The possible gametes are written along the top row and left column, and the boxes show all zygote combinations. A family tree describes pedigree analysis, not a Punnett square.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"The red blood cells of a person with genotype $I^AI^B$ carry both A-type and B-type sugars on their surface. This is an example of","image":"","options":["Incomplete dominance","Pleiotropy","Co-dominance","Polygenic inheritance"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When $I^A$ and $I^B$ are present together, each expresses its own sugar fully, so the heterozygote resembles both parents. This is co-dominance. It is not an intermediate phenotype, so it is not incomplete dominance.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the honey bee are correct?\n(i) Males are haploid with 16 chromosomes.\n(ii) Males produce sperms by meiosis.\n(iii) A drone has a father but no grandfather.\n(iv) A drone can have grandsons.\n(v) Queens and workers develop from fertilised eggs.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iv) and (v) are correct. Drones are haploid, so they form sperm by mitosis, not meiosis. A drone has no father but does have a grandfather, which is the reverse of statement (iii).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"During cell division, cytokinesis fails to occur after the telophase stage. As a result, the organism gains a whole extra set of chromosomes. This condition is most often seen in plants. What is it called?","image":"","options":["Aneuploidy","Polyploidy","Trisomy","Monosomy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When cytokinesis fails after telophase, a complete set of chromosomes is added, which is polyploidy. Aneuploidy, including trisomy and monosomy, is different: it is the gain or loss of individual chromosomes caused by failure of chromatid segregation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events in the correct chronological order:\nP. Chromosome movement during meiosis was worked out\nQ. Mendel published his work on inheritance\nR. Mendel's results were rediscovered independently by three scientists\nS. Mendel conducted hybridisation experiments on garden pea","image":"","options":["S, Q, P, R","Q, S, R, P","S, Q, R, P","S, R, Q, P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The correct order is the experiments (1856-1863), then publication (1865), then rediscovery (1900), then the working out of chromosome movement in meiosis (1902). A frequent error is to place the chromosomal observations before the rediscovery.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In humans, height does not occur as just two distinct classes (tall and short) but shows a whole range of values.\nStatement II: Human height is controlled by a single gene with two alleles, one of which is completely dominant over the other.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Human height varies across a gradient because it is a polygenic trait, influenced by several genes and by the environment. A single gene with complete dominance would give two distinct classes, as Mendel saw for tall and dwarf pea plants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\nA. Sutton and Boveri\nB. de Vries, Correns and von Tschermak\nC. T. H. Morgan\nD. Alfred Sturtevant\nList-II:\nI. Experimental verification of the chromosomal theory using fruit flies\nII. Mapping gene positions using recombination frequency\nIII. Independent rediscovery of Mendel's results in 1900\nIV. Noting that chromosome behaviour parallels gene behaviour\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The correct pairs are:\n- Sutton and Boveri drew the chromosome-gene parallel (IV).\n- de Vries, Correns and von Tschermak rediscovered Mendel's work in 1900 (III).\n- Morgan verified the theory in Drosophila (I).\n- Sturtevant mapped genes using recombination frequency (II).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A pink-flowered snapdragon is crossed with a white-flowered snapdragon. What are the expected phenotypes of the progeny?","image":"","options":["All pink","1 red : 1 white","1 pink : 1 white","1 red : 2 pink : 1 white"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rr × rr gives Rr and rr in equal proportion, that is, pink and white in a 1:1 ratio. No red (RR) plant can arise, because the white parent contributes only r.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $\\beta$ thalassemia is controlled by a single gene, HBB, on chromosome 11 of each parent.\nStatement II: In sickle-cell anaemia, heterozygous $Hb^AHb^S$ individuals show the diseased phenotype.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct as written. Statement II is wrong because only $Hb^SHb^S$ homozygotes show the disease. Heterozygotes appear unaffected but are carriers with the sickle-cell trait.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct according to the enzyme-based explanation of dominance?\n(a) The recessive trait is generally seen because the modified allele makes a non-functional enzyme or no enzyme.\n(b) The modified allele is generally the recessive allele.\n(c) The unmodified (functioning) allele represents the original phenotype and is the dominant allele.\n(d) Equivalent allele pairs, where the modified allele gives the same phenotype, are very rare.\n(e) A diploid organism carries only one copy of each gene.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b) and (c) are correct. Equivalent allele pairs are actually very common, so (d) is wrong. A diploid carries two copies of each gene as a pair of alleles, so (e) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In grasshopper, the somatic cells of males contain one chromosome fewer than those of females.\nReason (R): In the XO type of sex determination, males have only one X-chromosome besides the autosomes, whereas females have a pair of X-chromosomes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Both sexes carry the same autosomes. Male grasshoppers (XO) lack the second X that females (XX) have, so males have one chromosome fewer. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When true-breeding tall and dwarf pea plants are crossed, all F1 plants are tall.\nStatement II: In the F2 generation of this cross, some plants of intermediate height appear along with tall and dwarf plants.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"All F1 plants resemble the tall parent. In F2, plants are either tall or dwarf, with no blending, so no plants of in-between height appear.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In Morgan's experiments, yellow and white genes showed 1.3% recombination, while white and miniature wing genes showed 37.2% recombination. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["In the yellow-white cross, about 98.7% of the progeny carried parental gene combinations","In the white-miniature cross, non-parental combinations outnumbered parental combinations","A higher recombination frequency indicates that the two genes are located closer together","The low recombination between yellow and white shows that they lie on different chromosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"If 1.3% of the progeny are recombinants, the remaining 98.7% are parental types, which reflects very tight linkage. Even at 37.2% recombination, parental types (62.8%) are still the majority. Higher recombination means genes are farther apart, not closer, and low recombination indicates linkage on the same chromosome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a reason given for Mendel's work remaining unrecognised from 1865 until 1900?","image":"","options":["Communication was difficult in those days, so his work was not widely publicised","His idea of stable, discrete, non-blending factors did not satisfy contemporaries trying to explain the apparently continuous variation in nature","His experiments used too few plants for the results to be considered reliable","He could not provide physical proof of the existence of factors or say what they were made of"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mendel actually used a large sample size, and this was a strength of his work rather than a reason for neglect. The other three options, along with resistance to applying mathematics to biology, are the reasons NCERT lists.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A tall pea plant with yellow seeds (TtYy) is crossed with a tall pea plant with green seeds (Ttyy). What proportion of the offspring is expected to be tall with yellow seeds?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{16}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{9}{16}$","$\\frac{3}{8}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Tt × Tt gives $\\frac{3}{4}$ tall, and Yy × yy gives $\\frac{1}{2}$ yellow. With independent assortment, tall yellow = $\\frac{3}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{8}$. The answer $\\frac{9}{16}$ wrongly treats the cross as TtYy × TtYy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mendel selected 14 true-breeding pea varieties as 7 pairs, and the two varieties in each pair were similar except for one character with contrasting traits.\nStatement II: In garden pea, yellow is the dominant trait for both seed colour and pod colour.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly describes how Mendel paired his 14 varieties. Statement II is wrong because yellow is dominant for seed colour, but green is dominant for pod colour (green/yellow pods); the shared colour names are the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Red-green colour blindness occurs in about 8 per cent of males but only about 0.4 per cent of females.\nReason (R): The genes responsible for red-green colour blindness are on the X chromosome, and males have only one X chromosome.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A male with one mutant X allele is colour blind because he has no second X to mask it. A female needs two copies, which is why the frequency in females is far lower. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In Drosophila, Morgan found 1.3% recombination between genes white (w) and yellow (y), and 37.2% between white (w) and miniature wing (m). Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["y and w are more loosely linked than w and m","A higher recombination frequency means the two genes lie closer together on the chromosome","w and m lie farther apart on the chromosome than w and y","Because all three genes are on the X chromosome, none of them can recombine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Sturtevant used recombination frequency as a measure of distance between genes. The higher value for w–m (37.2%) means these genes are farther apart and more loosely linked than y–w. Genes on the same chromosome can still recombine.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In a species of grasshopper, the somatic cells of a female contain 24 chromosomes. Which of the following correctly gives the chromosome number in the somatic cells of a male, and the sperm produced by that male?","image":"","options":["Male somatic cells: 24; all sperm: 12","Male somatic cells: 23; all sperm: 11","Male somatic cells: 23; half the sperm have 12 and half have 11","Male somatic cells: 22; half the sperm have 12 and half have 10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The female (XX) has 22 autosomes plus 2 X, so the male (XO) has 22 autosomes plus 1 X, which is 23. Each sperm receives 11 autosomes. The single X goes to only half the sperm, giving 12 chromosomes in those sperm and 11 in the rest.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly states Mendel's Law of Independent Assortment?","image":"","options":["Alleles of a gene blend in the hybrid and separate again in the next generation","In a dissimilar pair of factors, one member of the pair masks the expression of the other","When two pairs of traits are combined in a hybrid, segregation of one pair of characters is independent of the other pair","Genes located on the same chromosome always assort independently of each other"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The law states that each pair of characters segregates without being influenced by the other pair in a dihybrid. Option 2 describes the Law of Dominance, and Mendel found no blending of alleles. Genes on the same chromosome tend to be inherited together (linkage), so they do not always assort independently.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following ABO genotypes are heterozygous?\n$I^AI^B$, $I^Ai$, $I^BI^B$, $ii$, $I^Bi$, $I^AI^A$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$I^AI^B$, $I^Ai$ and $I^Bi$ carry two different alleles, so three genotypes are heterozygous. $I^BI^B$, $ii$ and $I^AI^A$ are homozygous. Students often wrongly leave out $I^AI^B$ because it shows a distinct phenotype.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A male honey bee (drone) has no father but can have grandsons.\nReason (R): Drones develop from unfertilised eggs by parthenogenesis and are haploid.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A drone comes from an unfertilised egg, so it has a mother but no father. Its sperm fertilise eggs that become females, and those daughters' unfertilised eggs become drones, which are its grandsons. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"From the F2 of Mendel's dihybrid cross RRYY × rryy, a plant with round, yellow seeds is picked at random. What is the probability that it is heterozygous at both loci (RrYy)?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{9}$","$\\frac{4}{16}$","$\\frac{4}{9}$","$\\frac{2}{9}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Round yellow plants occupy 9 of the 16 boxes, and RrYy occupies 4 of these. The conditional probability is therefore $\\frac{4}{9}$. The answer $\\frac{4}{16}$ is RrYy's share of all F2 plants, not of the round yellow plants only.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Henking (1891) traced a specific nuclear structure through spermatogenesis in a few insects and named it the 'X body'. What proportion of the sperm received this structure after spermatogenesis?","image":"","options":["25 per cent","50 per cent","75 per cent","100 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Henking found that half of the sperm received the X body and the other half did not. He could not explain why. The structure was later identified as the X-chromosome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a reason why Drosophila melanogaster was found suitable for Morgan's genetic studies?","image":"","options":["It can be grown on a simple synthetic medium in the laboratory","It completes its life cycle in about two weeks","Male and female flies look alike and cannot be easily told apart","A single mating produces a large number of progeny"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"One advantage of Drosophila is a clear differentiation of the sexes, so males and females are easily distinguished. The statement that they look alike is therefore false. The other three options are genuine advantages given in NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A woman with normal vision, whose father was red-green colour blind, marries a colour-blind man. Which of the following correctly predicts their children?","image":"","options":["All sons will be colour blind and no daughter will be colour blind","About 50% of the sons and about 50% of the daughters will be colour blind","All daughters will be carriers and none of the children will be colour blind","About 25% of the sons will be colour blind and all daughters will have normal vision"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The woman must be a carrier, having received her father's affected X. Each son therefore has a 50% chance of inheriting her mutant X. Each daughter receives the father's mutant X and has a 50% chance of a mutant X from the mother, so half the daughters are expected to be colour blind and the rest carriers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Phenylketonuria is caused by a mutation in the gene coding for which enzyme?","image":"","options":["Tyrosinase","Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Phenylpyruvate decarboxylase","Homogentisate oxidase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Phenylketonuria results from a single-gene mutation affecting phenylalanine hydroxylase, the enzyme that converts phenylalanine into tyrosine. Phenylpyruvic acid is a product that accumulates in the disease; it is not the deficient enzyme.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: $\\alpha$ Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes, HBA1 and HBA2, on chromosome 16.\nStatement II: $\\beta$ Thalassemia is controlled by a single gene, HBB, on chromosome 11 and is a qualitative defect in which an incorrectly functioning globin is made.\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The gene locations in both statements are correct. However, thalassemia is a quantitative problem, meaning too few globin chains are made. The qualitative defect of an incorrectly functioning globin describes sickle-cell anaemia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In humans, the genetic makeup of the sperm decides the sex of the child.\nReason (R): Human males produce two kinds of sperm, X-bearing and Y-bearing, in equal proportion, while females produce only X-bearing ova.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Because all ova carry X, the sex of the zygote depends on whether an X- or a Y-bearing sperm fertilises the ovum. This male heterogamety is exactly why the sperm determines sex.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"An F1 pea plant of genotype RrYy (round, yellow seeds) forms gametes by meiosis. Which of the following can NOT be the genotype of one of its gametes?","image":"","options":["RY","rY","Ry","Rr"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Alleles of a pair segregate, so each gamete gets only one allele of each gene: RY, Ry, rY or ry. 'Rr' has both alleles of the seed-shape gene and no allele for seed colour, so it cannot be a gamete.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the haplodiploid sex-determination system of the honey bee is correct?","image":"","options":["Drones develop from fertilised eggs and have 32 chromosomes","Drones produce sperms by mitosis and have no father","Worker bees are haploid and have 16 chromosomes","A drone can have sons but can never have grandsons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Drones develop from unfertilised eggs by parthenogenesis, so they are haploid (16 chromosomes), have no father and form sperms by mitosis. They cannot have sons but can have grandsons. Queens and workers come from fertilised eggs and are diploid (32 chromosomes).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"UV radiation can bring about changes in the DNA of organisms. On this basis, UV radiation is classified as a:","image":"","options":["Mutagen","Antigen","Allergen","Teratogen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Physical and chemical factors that induce mutations are called mutagens, and NCERT cites UV radiation as an example. The other terms describe agents with different effects, not agents defined by causing DNA alteration.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (disorder) with List-II (cause) and choose the correct option.\nList-I\nA. Down's syndrome\nB. Turner's syndrome\nC. Klinefelter's syndrome\nD. Sickle-cell anaemia\nList-II\n(i) Karyotype 47, XXY\n(ii) Valine replaces glutamic acid at the sixth position of the $\\beta$-globin chain\n(iii) Trisomy of chromosome 21\n(iv) Karyotype 45, X0","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Down's syndrome is trisomy 21. Turner's syndrome is 45, X0, and Klinefelter's syndrome is 47, XXY. Sickle-cell anaemia results from a Glu to Val substitution at position 6 of the $\\beta$ chain. The common trap is swapping the Turner and Klinefelter karyotypes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In sickle-cell anaemia, which change occurs at the sixth position of the $\\beta$-globin chain of haemoglobin?","image":"","options":["Valine is replaced by glutamic acid","Glutamic acid is replaced by valine","Glutamic acid is replaced by lysine","Valine is replaced by alanine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The normal $\\beta$ chain has glutamic acid (Glu) at position 6. In sickle-cell anaemia it is replaced by valine (Val). The common trap is reversing the direction of the substitution.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Morgan crossed yellow-bodied, white-eyed female Drosophila with brown-bodied, red-eyed males and intercrossed the F1. The F2 ratio deviated significantly from 9:3:3:1. What is the best explanation?","image":"","options":["The two genes showed incomplete dominance, so the heterozygotes were intermediate","The two genes lie on the X chromosome and are physically associated, so parental combinations far outnumbered non-parental ones","The two genes lie on different autosomes and assort independently at meiosis","Yellow body colour and white eye colour are controlled by multiple alleles of one gene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Both genes are located on the X chromosome, so they are linked and tend to be inherited together. This raises the proportion of parental types and distorts the 9:3:3:1 ratio. Independent assortment, the trap in option 3, is exactly what would produce 9:3:3:1.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In Morgan's dihybrid crosses in Drosophila, the genes white (w) and yellow (y) showed 1.3 per cent recombination, while white (w) and miniature wing (m) showed 37.2 per cent recombination. Which inference is correct?","image":"","options":["w and m are more tightly linked than y and w","y and w must lie on different chromosomes because they recombine","Recombination frequency has no relation to the distance between genes","y and w lie closer together on the X chromosome than w and m do"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A lower recombination frequency means tighter linkage and a shorter distance between genes. Sturtevant used this principle to map genes. Hence y and w are closer together than w and m, and all three genes are on the X chromosome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"The genetic disorder caused by trisomy of chromosome 21 was first described by:","image":"","options":["Henking in 1891","Langdon Down in 1866","Walter Sutton in 1902","Thomas Hunt Morgan in 1910"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Down's syndrome is named after Langdon Down, who described it in 1866. Henking named the 'X body', and Sutton and Morgan worked on the chromosomal theory and linkage.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the human ABO blood group system is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Three alleles of gene I give six possible genotypes","When $I^A$ and $I^B$ occur together, both types of sugar are present on the RBC surface","A single individual can carry all three alleles $I^A$, $I^B$ and $i$","Allele $i$ does not produce any sugar, so $I^A i$ individuals have blood group A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A diploid person carries only two alleles of a gene. This is why multiple alleles can be detected only through population studies. The other statements correctly describe the genotypes, co-dominance and the recessive i allele.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the F2 of a monohybrid cross showing complete dominance, the phenotypic ratio (3:1) differs from the genotypic ratio (1:2:1).\nReason (R): Homozygous dominant and heterozygous individuals express the same phenotype because the dominant allele masks the recessive one.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"TT (1/4) and Tt (1/2) both appear tall, so these two genotypic classes merge into a single phenotypic class (3/4). That merging is exactly what turns 1:2:1 into 3:1, so R explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a pea plant with round, yellow seeds (RRYY) is crossed with one having wrinkled, green seeds (rryy), all F1 seeds are round and yellow.\nReason (R): Round seed shape is dominant over wrinkled, and yellow seed colour is dominant over green.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The F1 has the genotype RrYy. Because R and Y are dominant, every F1 seed shows the round, yellow phenotype. So R directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"For how many years did Gregor Mendel carry out hybridisation experiments on garden pea before proposing the laws of inheritance?","image":"","options":["Five years (1858-1863)","Seven years (1856-1863)","Ten years (1856-1866)","Seven years (1865-1872)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mendel's pea hybridisation experiments ran for seven years, from 1856 to 1863. The year 1865 is when he published his work, not when the experiments began.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Sutton and Boveri\nB. T.H. Morgan\nC. Alfred Sturtevant\nD. de Vries, Correns and von Tschermak\nList-II\n(i) Independently rediscovered Mendel's results in 1900\n(ii) Used recombination frequency to map the positions of genes on a chromosome\n(iii) Noted that the behaviour of chromosomes runs parallel to the behaviour of genes\n(iv) Coined the terms linkage and recombination\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Sutton and Boveri linked chromosome movement to Mendel's laws. Morgan named linkage and recombination, and his student Sturtevant built genetic maps from recombination frequencies. The three botanists rediscovered Mendel's work in 1900. A common trap is crediting the mapping to Morgan instead of Sturtevant.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In Mendel's experiments, a 'true-breeding line' refers to a plant line that:","image":"","options":["Is produced by crossing two different varieties in every generation","Shows a blend of both parental traits in every generation","Has undergone continuous self-pollination and shows stable inheritance and expression of a trait for several generations","Always expresses the recessive form of every character"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A true-breeding line results from continuous self-pollination and expresses the same trait stably for several generations. Repeated cross-pollination would produce hybrids, not true-breeding lines.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A pea plant of genotype RrYy (round, yellow seeds) produces gametes. What is the expected frequency of gametes carrying the alleles r and Y together?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{1}{16}$","$\\frac{1}{8}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"R and r each go to half the gametes, and Y and y each go to half, independently of each other. So the four gamete types RY, Ry, rY and ry each occur at a frequency of $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4}$. The value $\\frac{1}{16}$ is the frequency of one zygote combination, not of one gamete type.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about sickle-cell anaemia are correct?\n(a) Heterozygous ($Hb^AHb^S$) individuals show the full diseased phenotype.\n(b) The mutation is a single base change in the sixth codon of the $\\beta$-globin gene, from GAG to GUG.\n(c) The mutant haemoglobin polymerises under low oxygen tension, making the RBC sickle shaped.\n(d) It is an X-linked recessive trait.\n(e) Two carrier parents can have an affected child.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (b), (c) and (e) are correct. Heterozygotes appear apparently unaffected and are carriers, so (a) is wrong. The gene is autosomal, not X-linked, so (d) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the human ABO blood group system is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Alleles $I^A$ and $I^B$ produce slightly different forms of a sugar polymer that protrudes from the RBC plasma membrane","Allele $i$ does not produce any sugar on the RBC surface","In an $I^AI^B$ individual, both A and B types of sugar are present on the RBCs","Allele $I^A$ is incompletely dominant over allele $i$, so $I^Ai$ individuals show an intermediate amount of sugar"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"$I^A$ and $I^B$ are completely dominant over $i$. Because $i$ makes no sugar, only $I^A$ is expressed in $I^Ai$, and the phenotype is simply group A. Only $I^A$ and $I^B$ together show co-dominance.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which phenomenon results when cytokinesis fails after the telophase stage of cell division, giving an increase in a whole set of chromosomes?","image":"","options":["Aneuploidy","Trisomy","Polyploidy","Monosomy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When cytokinesis fails after telophase, a whole extra set of chromosomes stays in one cell. This is polyploidy, which is often seen in plants. Aneuploidy, trisomy and monosomy involve the gain or loss of single chromosomes, not whole sets.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"The genes HBA1 and HBA2, whose mutation or deletion causes $\\alpha$ thalassemia, are located on which human chromosome?","image":"","options":["Chromosome 11","Chromosome 21","Chromosome 16","X chromosome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$\\alpha$ thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes, HBA1 and HBA2, on chromosome 16. Chromosome 11 carries HBB, the gene involved in $\\beta$ thalassemia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the F2 of a dihybrid cross RRYY × rryy, the genotypic ratio is 1:2:1:2:4:2:1:2:1, not 9:3:3:1.\nStatement II: Among the F2 plants with round, green seeds, one-third will produce only round, green-seeded progeny on self-pollination.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the genotypic ratio is (1:2:1) × (1:2:1), which gives nine genotype classes, while 9:3:3:1 is only the phenotypic ratio. Statement II is also correct: round green plants are RRyy ($\\frac{1}{16}$) and Rryy ($\\frac{2}{16}$), so only RRyy, one-third of them, breeds true.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Three genes P, Q and R lie on the same chromosome. The recombination frequencies between them are: P-Q = 4%, P-R = 26% and Q-R = 30%. Using recombination frequency as a measure of distance (as Sturtevant did), what is the correct order of these genes on the chromosome?","image":"","options":["P - Q - R","P - R - Q","Q - P - R","Q - R - P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The two outermost genes are separated by the largest distance, which is Q-R at 30%. P must lie between them because Q-P (4%) plus P-R (26%) equals 30%. The order is therefore Q-P-R.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Pea seeds of genotype Bb are round, yet the starch grains in them are of intermediate size between those of BB and bb seeds.\nReason (R): Dominance is an autonomous feature of a gene and does not depend on the phenotype being examined.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"If seed shape is taken as the phenotype, B appears dominant. If starch grain size is taken as the phenotype, B and b show incomplete dominance. This shows that dominance depends on the phenotype chosen and is not autonomous, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about polygenic inheritance is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Polygenic traits are generally controlled by three or more genes","The environment can also influence the expression of a polygenic trait","The phenotype depends on which particular dominant allele is present, not on how many dominant alleles are present","The effect of each contributing allele is additive"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In polygenic inheritance, the number of contributing alleles decides the phenotype, because their effects are additive. Which specific gene carries them does not matter, so AABbcc and aaBbCC give the same shade.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Mendel's results on the inheritance of characters were independently rediscovered in 1900 by:","image":"","options":["Sutton, Boveri and Morgan","de Vries, Correns and von Tschermak","Morgan, Sturtevant and Punnett","Henking, Sutton and de Vries"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hugo de Vries, Correns and von Tschermak independently rediscovered Mendel's work in 1900. Sutton and Boveri later proposed the chromosomal basis of inheritance, and Morgan verified it experimentally.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In a pea plant, T and t control tallness and dwarfness respectively. T and t are best described as:","image":"","options":["Two different genes for two different characters","Alleles, i.e., slightly different forms of the same gene coding for a pair of contrasting traits","Homologous chromosomes","Two phenotypes of the same genotype"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Genes that code for a pair of contrasting traits of one character are alleles of each other. This is why the same letter is used in capital and lower case. They are not separate genes, chromosomes or phenotypes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $\\alpha$ Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes, HBA1 and HBA2, located on chromosome 11.\nStatement II: Thalassemia is a quantitative defect in which too few globin chains are made, whereas sickle-cell anaemia is a qualitative defect.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"HBA1 and HBA2 lie on chromosome 16. Chromosome 11 carries HBB, the gene for $\\beta$ thalassemia, so Statement I is wrong. Statement II correctly contrasts reduced globin synthesis in thalassemia with the faulty globin of sickle-cell anaemia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which event in meiosis provides the chromosomal basis for Mendel's law of independent assortment?","image":"","options":["Separation of sister chromatids during anaphase II","Independent alignment and separation of different homologous chromosome pairs during meiosis I","Replication of chromosomes during the S phase before meiosis","Condensation of chromosomes during prophase II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In meiosis I, each homologous pair orients and separates independently of every other pair. As a result, alleles on different chromosomes assort independently. Sister chromatids separate in anaphase II, but that step does not create new combinations of different chromosome pairs.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Organism)\n(a) Grasshopper\n(b) Human\n(c) Birds\n(d) Honey bee\nList-II (Sex-determining mechanism)\n(i) Female heterogamety (ZW female, ZZ male)\n(ii) XO type; males have one chromosome fewer than females\n(iii) Haplodiploidy based on number of chromosome sets\n(iv) XY type; male heterogamety with equal chromosome number in both sexes\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Grasshopper males are XO, so they have one fewer chromosome than XX females. Humans are XY with equal chromosome numbers in both sexes. Female birds are ZW, which is female heterogamety. Honey bees follow a haplodiploid system.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Genotype)\nA. BB (pea)\nB. Bb (pea)\nC. bb (pea)\nD. Rr (snapdragon)\nList-II (Phenotype)\nI. Pink flowers\nII. Round seed with intermediate-sized starch grains\nIII. Wrinkled seed with smaller starch grains\nIV. Round seed with large starch grains","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"BB gives round seeds with large starch grains, and Bb gives round seeds with intermediate grains. bb gives wrinkled seeds with small grains, and the snapdragon heterozygote Rr is pink. The trap is assuming Bb grains match BB simply because both seeds are round.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Turner's syndrome is an example of aneuploidy.\nReason (R): Aneuploidy results from failure of cytokinesis after telophase of cell division.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Turner's syndrome (loss of one X) is aneuploidy, so A is true. However, aneuploidy arises from failure of chromatid segregation, not failure of cytokinesis, so R is false. Failure of cytokinesis causes polyploidy instead.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"The chromosomal constitution of a female with Turner's syndrome is:","image":"","options":["47, XXY","47, XXX","46, XY","45, XO"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Turner's syndrome results from the absence of one X chromosome, giving 45 chromosomes with XO. The karyotype 47, XXY is Klinefelter's syndrome, a common confusion.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Change in a single base pair of DNA\nB. Deletion or insertion of base pairs\nC. Loss or gain of a segment of DNA in a chromatid\nD. UV radiation\nList-II\nI. Mutagen\nII. Frame-shift mutation\nIII. Point mutation\nIV. Chromosomal aberration","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The matches are as follows:\n- A single base change is a point mutation.\n- Inserting or deleting base pairs shifts the reading frame, causing a frame-shift mutation.\n- Loss or gain of a DNA segment alters the chromosome, which is a chromosomal aberration.\n- UV radiation is a mutagen.\nThe common trap is swapping point and frame-shift mutations.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In Morgan's Drosophila crosses, the genes white and miniature wing showed far more recombination than the genes yellow and white.\nReason (R): The farther apart two genes lie on the same chromosome, the higher the recombination frequency between them.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The white-miniature pair showed 37.2% recombination, compared with only 1.3% for yellow-white. This is because recombination frequency rises with the distance between linked genes, which is the very basis of Sturtevant's mapping. So both statements are true and R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Phenylketonuria is an example of pleiotropy.\nReason (R): A single gene mutation in phenylketonuria disturbs a metabolic pathway and causes both mental retardation and reduced hair and skin pigmentation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Pleiotropy usually arises because one gene affects a metabolic pathway that feeds into several phenotypes. In phenylketonuria, one mutated gene produces more than one effect: mental retardation and reduced pigmentation. R therefore explains A directly.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In the dog flower (Antirrhinum sp.), a true-breeding red-flowered plant (RR) is crossed with a true-breeding white-flowered plant (rr). What is the flower colour of the F1 plants?","image":"","options":["All red","All white","All pink","Red and white in a 1:1 ratio"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"R is not completely dominant over r, so the heterozygote Rr has an intermediate pink colour. The F1 resembles neither parent fully, which is the hallmark of incomplete dominance.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I (Scientist)\nA. Henking\nB. Walter Sutton\nC. Alfred Sturtevant\nD. Reginald C. Punnett\nList-II (Contribution)\nI. Graphical method for calculating the probability of offspring genotypes\nII. Traced an 'X body' through spermatogenesis in insects\nIII. Mapped gene positions using recombination frequency\nIV. United chromosomal segregation with Mendelian principles\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Henking observed the X body, and Sutton proposed the chromosomal theory of inheritance. Sturtevant used recombination frequency to map genes, and Punnett devised the Punnett square.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assume human skin colour is controlled by three genes A, B and C, where each dominant allele adds equally to darkness (additive effect). Two individuals of genotype AaBbCc marry. What fraction of their children is expected to have the same intermediate skin colour as the parents, that is, exactly three dominant alleles?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{64}$","$\\frac{5}{16}$","$\\frac{15}{64}$","$\\frac{3}{8}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each parent passes 3 alleles, giving 6 allele positions with a 1/2 chance each of being dominant. The number of ways to get exactly 3 dominant alleles is $^6C_3 = 20$ out of 64, which equals $\\frac{5}{16}$. The value 15/64 is the fraction for 2 or 4 dominant alleles, a common trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the F2 of a cross between true-breeding tall and dwarf pea plants, a tall plant cannot be identified as TT or Tt simply by looking at it.\nReason (R): In a heterozygote, the dominant allele T alone is expressed, so Tt plants are phenotypically identical to TT plants.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Because T dominates t, Tt plants look exactly like TT plants. A tall phenotype therefore does not reveal the genotype, which is why Mendel needed the test cross.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Down's syndrome\nB. Klinefelter's syndrome\nC. Turner's syndrome\nD. Polyploidy\nList-II\nI. Rudimentary ovaries\nII. Increase in a whole set of chromosomes\nIII. Gynaecomastia\nIV. Broad palm with characteristic palm crease\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Down's syndrome shows a broad palm with a characteristic crease, and Klinefelter's males show breast development (gynaecomastia). Turner's females have rudimentary ovaries, and polyploidy adds whole chromosome sets.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are autosomal recessive Mendelian disorders?\nSickle-cell anaemia, Haemophilia, Phenylketonuria, Myotonic dystrophy, Thalassemia, Colour blindness, Down's syndrome","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Sickle-cell anaemia, phenylketonuria and thalassemia are autosomal recessive. Haemophilia and colour blindness are X-linked recessive, myotonic dystrophy is autosomal dominant, and Down's syndrome is a chromosomal disorder.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In Mendel's dihybrid cross between true-breeding round yellow (RRYY) and wrinkled green (rryy) pea plants, the F1 was self-pollinated. What fraction of the F2 plants is expected to bear wrinkled green seeds?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{16}$","$\\frac{1}{16}$","$\\frac{9}{16}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Wrinkled green plants are the double recessive class rryy. Its frequency is $\\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{16}$, the '1' of the 9:3:3:1 ratio. $\\frac{1}{4}$ is the fraction of green (or of wrinkled) seeds taken alone.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are described in NCERT as autosomal recessive disorders?\nSickle-cell anaemia, Phenylketonuria, Haemophilia, Colour blindness, Thalassemia, Myotonic dystrophy","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Sickle-cell anaemia, phenylketonuria and thalassemia are autosomal recessive. Haemophilia and colour blindness are X-linked (sex-linked) recessive, and myotonic dystrophy is NCERT's example of an autosomal dominant trait.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Gene I controlling ABO blood groups has three alleles. How many different genotypes and how many different phenotypes are possible in the human population?","image":"","options":["3 genotypes and 4 phenotypes","6 genotypes and 4 phenotypes","4 genotypes and 6 phenotypes","9 genotypes and 4 phenotypes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Three alleles taken two at a time give six genotypes: $I^AI^A$, $I^AI^B$, $I^Ai$, $I^BI^B$, $I^Bi$ and $ii$. These produce only four blood groups (A, B, AB and O) because $I^AI^A$ and $I^Ai$ both give A, and $I^BI^B$ and $I^Bi$ both give B. The value 9 is a trap from counting ordered parent combinations.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A diploid cell has two pairs of homologous chromosomes. One pair is long (one orange, one yellow) and the other pair is short (one green, one red). At metaphase I, the two pairs can orient in two different ways relative to each other. Considering both orientations, how many different combinations of chromosomes can the resulting gametes carry?","image":"","options":["2","4","6","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each pair orients independently at meiosis I. One orientation yields orange+green and yellow+red gametes, and the other yields orange+red and yellow+green gametes. That gives 4 combinations in total, which is the chromosomal basis of independent assortment.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In garden pea, starch synthesis in seeds is controlled by a gene with alleles B and b. What is the seed phenotype of a bb homozygote after maturation?","image":"","options":["Round seeds with large starch grains","Wrinkled seeds with smaller starch grains","Round seeds with intermediate-sized starch grains","Wrinkled seeds with large starch grains"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"bb plants synthesise starch less efficiently, so they form smaller starch grains and their seeds become wrinkled on maturation. Large grains and round seeds belong to BB.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A woman with normal vision whose father was colour blind marries a colour-blind man. What fraction of their daughters is expected to be colour blind?","image":"","options":["0","1/4","1/2","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The woman must be a carrier ($X^cX$), since she received $X^c$ from her colour-blind father. Every daughter receives $X^c$ from the colour-blind father, and from her mother she receives either $X^c$ or $X$. So half the daughters are colour blind and half are carriers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In sickle-cell anaemia, which amino acid replaces glutamic acid at the sixth position of the $\\beta$-globin chain of haemoglobin?","image":"","options":["Lysine","Valine","Glycine","Alanine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A single base substitution in the sixth codon of the $\\beta$-globin gene places valine where glutamic acid normally sits. This changes the haemoglobin molecule and causes the disease.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A woman with blood group AB marries a man with blood group B whose mother had blood group O. What phenotypic ratio of blood groups is expected among their children?","image":"","options":["A : AB : B = 1 : 1 : 2","A : B = 1 : 1","A : AB : B : O = 1 : 1 : 1 : 1","AB : B = 1 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The man's mother was $ii$, so he must carry $i$ and his genotype is $I^Bi$. The cross $I^AI^B \\times I^Bi$ gives $I^AI^B$ (AB), $I^Ai$ (A), $I^BI^B$ (B) and $I^Bi$ (B), which is A : AB : B = 1 : 1 : 2. No O child is possible because the AB mother cannot pass on $i$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about thalassemia is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$\\alpha$ Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes, HBA1 and HBA2, on chromosome 16 of each parent","In $\\alpha$ Thalassemia, the more of the four genes that are affected, the fewer $\\alpha$-globin molecules are produced","$\\beta$ Thalassemia occurs only when both copies of the HBB gene are mutated; a mutation in one copy has no role","Thalassemia is an autosome-linked recessive disorder that can be transmitted when both parents are unaffected carriers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$\\beta$ Thalassemia occurs due to mutation of one or both HBB genes on chromosome 11, so the claim that it needs both copies is wrong. The other three statements match the description of thalassemia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In humans, the genetic makeup of the sperm determines the sex of the child, and every pregnancy carries a 50 per cent probability of a male child.\nStatement II: In birds, the egg determines the sex of the chick because the female produces two types of gametes with respect to sex chromosomes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Human males are heterogametic and produce X-bearing and Y-bearing sperm in equal numbers, so the sperm decides the child's sex. Female birds are ZW and produce Z-bearing and W-bearing eggs, which is female heterogamety, so the egg decides the chick's sex.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A diploid plant has the genotype AaBBCcDd. All four genes are on different chromosomes and assort independently. How many types of gametes can this plant produce, and how many different genotypes are expected in the progeny if it is self-pollinated?","image":"","options":["8 gamete types; 27 genotypes","16 gamete types; 81 genotypes","8 gamete types; 81 genotypes","16 gamete types; 27 genotypes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Only three loci (A, C, D) are heterozygous, so there are $2^3 = 8$ gamete types. On selfing, each heterozygous locus gives 3 genotypes while BB gives only BB, so there are $3^3 = 27$ genotypes. The trap is counting the homozygous BB locus as if it were heterozygous.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct with respect to Mendel's Law of Segregation?\n(i) A homozygous parent produces gametes that are all similar.\n(ii) A heterozygous parent produces two kinds of gametes, each carrying one allele, in equal proportion.\n(iii) The two alleles in an F1 hybrid blend partially and are recovered in modified form in the F2.\n(iv) The alleles of a pair separate during gamete formation by meiosis, so each gamete receives only one allele.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iv) are correct. Statement (iii) is wrong because alleles never blend: both parental traits reappear unchanged in the F2.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about sex determination in birds is correct?","image":"","options":["Males are ZW and females are ZZ","Females produce two kinds of gametes, so the egg decides the sex of the chick","Males are heterogametic, as in humans","Females have one chromosome fewer than males"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Female birds are ZW and produce Z-bearing and W-bearing eggs, so the ovum determines the chick's sex. This is female heterogamety. Males are ZZ, and both sexes have the same total chromosome number.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Organism)\nA. Grasshopper\nB. Drosophila\nC. Fowl (bird)\nD. Honey bee\nList-II (Sex-determining mechanism)\nI. Female heterogamety (ZW/ZZ)\nII. Haplodiploidy\nIII. XO type\nIV. XY type\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The grasshopper shows the XO type and Drosophila the XY type. Birds show ZW female heterogamety. Honey bees are haplodiploid: females are diploid and males are haploid.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In a family pedigree, two unaffected parents have three children: one affected daughter and two unaffected sons. Which mode of inheritance best explains this pattern?","image":"","options":["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","Y-linked"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An affected child of two unaffected parents means both parents are carriers of a recessive allele, so the trait cannot be dominant. An affected daughter under X-linked recessive inheritance would need an affected father, and Y-linked traits never appear in females. This leaves autosomal recessive, as seen in sickle-cell anaemia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In the three-gene additive model of skin colour, how many of the following genotypes would have exactly the same skin colour as an AaBbCc individual?\nAABbcc, aaBBCc, AaBbCC, AAbbCc, AabbCc, AaBBcc","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Since the effects are additive, any genotype with exactly three dominant alleles matches AaBbCc. Four genotypes qualify: AABbcc, aaBBCc, AAbbCc and AaBBcc. AaBbCC has four dominant alleles and AabbCc has two, so they are excluded.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a person with genotype $I^AI^B$, both alleles express their own type of sugar on the RBC membrane.\nStatement II: Allele $i$ is dominant over allele $I^B$ because it does not produce any sugar.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I describes co-dominance and is correct. Statement II is incorrect: because $i$ makes no sugar, it is recessive, and in $I^Bi$ only $I^B$ is expressed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In pea, BB seeds have large starch grains and bb seeds have small starch grains. Bb seeds are round like BB seeds but have starch grains of intermediate size. If starch grain size is taken as the phenotype, alleles B and b show:","image":"","options":["Complete dominance of B over b","Incomplete dominance","Co-dominance","Multiple allelism"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The heterozygote's starch grain size lies between the two homozygotes, which is incomplete dominance. For seed shape the same alleles show complete dominance, so dominance depends on the phenotype examined.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cross in pea for height, T = tall, t = dwarf) with List-II (expected ratio of progeny).\nList-I\nA. $Tt \\times Tt$ (phenotypes)\nB. $Tt \\times Tt$ (genotypes)\nC. $Tt \\times tt$ (phenotypes)\nD. $TT \\times tt$ (phenotypes)\nList-II\nI. 1 : 2 : 1\nII. All tall\nIII. 3 : 1\nIV. 1 : 1\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Selfing Tt gives a 3:1 phenotypic and 1:2:1 genotypic ratio. A test cross of Tt gives 1 tall : 1 dwarf. TT × tt gives only Tt, so all plants are tall. The trap is swapping the phenotypic and genotypic ratios of the F2.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assume human skin colour is controlled by three genes A, B and C, where the dominant alleles add to dark colour. Which genotype will have the lightest skin colour?","image":"","options":["AABBCC","AaBbCc","aabbCC","aabbcc"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In this additive model, each dominant allele adds darkness. The genotype with no dominant alleles (aabbcc) is therefore the lightest, and AABBCC is the darkest.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"The graphical method used to work out the probability of all possible genotypes of offspring, with gametes written along the top row and left column, was developed by:","image":"","options":["Gregor Mendel","Thomas Hunt Morgan","Reginald C. Punnett","Walter Sutton"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The Punnett square is named after the British geneticist Reginald C. Punnett, who developed it. Mendel did the pea crosses but did not devise this diagram.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In the human ABO blood group system, which statement correctly describes the allele $i$ of gene I?","image":"","options":["It produces no sugar polymer on the surface of red blood cells","It produces a sugar slightly different from the one produced by $I^A$","It is co-dominant with $I^A$","It is dominant over $I^B$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$I^A$ and $I^B$ each produce a slightly different sugar, but $i$ produces no sugar. For this reason $i$ is recessive to both $I^A$ and $I^B$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the F2 of a dihybrid cross RRYY × rryy, the genotypic ratio is the same as the phenotypic ratio, i.e., 9:3:3:1.\nStatement II: The F2 plants with round yellow seeds belong to four different genotypes.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The F2 has 9 genotypes in the ratio 1:2:1:2:4:2:1:2:1, so it is not 9:3:3:1. The round yellow class includes RRYY, RRYy, RrYY and RrYy, which are four genotypes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In snapdragon (Antirrhinum), a true-breeding red-flowered plant (RR) is crossed with a true-breeding white-flowered plant (rr). The F1 plants are then self-pollinated. What fraction of the F2 plants will bear pink flowers?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{3}{4}$","$\\frac{1}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The F1 is Rr (pink). Selfing gives RR : Rr : rr in the ratio 1 : 2 : 1, and because R is incompletely dominant, every Rr plant is pink, so half the F2 is pink. The value $\\frac{3}{4}$ is the trap: it applies only when one allele is completely dominant.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A monohybrid cross between true-breeding tall and dwarf pea plants yields 400 F2 plants.\nStatement I: About 200 of these F2 plants are expected to breed true on self-pollination, as predicted by the expansion $(\\frac{1}{2}T + \\frac{1}{2}t)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}TT + \\frac{1}{2}Tt + \\frac{1}{4}tt$.\nStatement II: Every tall F2 plant breeds true on self-pollination because tallness is the dominant trait.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The homozygotes (100 TT and 100 tt) breed true, giving about 200 plants, so Statement I is correct. Statement II is wrong because the Tt tall plants (half of all F2) segregate to give dwarf progeny on selfing.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A son born to a carrier woman and a normal man has a 50% chance of being colour blind.\n(ii) A daughter of a colour-blind father and a non-carrier normal mother will be colour blind.\n(iii) Colour blindness occurs in about 8% of males but only about 0.4% of females.\n(iv) For a female to be haemophilic, her mother must be at least a carrier and her father must be haemophilic.\n(v) A haemophilic father passes the disease allele to all his sons.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iii) and (iv) are correct, giving three. In (ii), the daughter receives one normal X from her mother, so she is only a carrier. In (v), a father gives his Y chromosome, not his X, to his sons, so an X-linked allele cannot pass from father to son.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the F2 generation of a cross between true-breeding red and white snapdragons, the phenotypic ratio is identical to the genotypic ratio.\nReason (R): Allele R is incompletely dominant over r, so the heterozygote Rr can be distinguished from both RR and rr.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rr (pink) looks different from RR (red) and rr (white), so each genotype has its own phenotype. Both ratios are therefore 1:2:1, and R correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the chromosomal condition in a grasshopper, which shows the XO type of sex determination?","image":"","options":["Males have XY and females have XX, so both sexes have the same total chromosome number","Males have one X-chromosome besides the autosomes and females have a pair of X-chromosomes, so males have one chromosome fewer","Females have one X-chromosome and males have a pair of X-chromosomes, so females have one chromosome fewer","Females have ZW and males have ZZ chromosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In the XO type, the male carries a single X with no partner sex chromosome, while the female is XX. The two sexes therefore differ by one chromosome. In the XY and ZW systems, by contrast, both sexes have the same total number.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are characteristic features of an individual with Down's syndrome?\nShort stature; furrowed tongue; gynaecomastia; partially open mouth; rudimentary ovaries; broad palm with characteristic palm crease; tall stature","image":"","options":["3","5","6","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Four features belong to Down's syndrome: short stature, furrowed tongue, partially open mouth and a broad palm with a characteristic crease. Gynaecomastia and tall stature are features of Klinefelter's syndrome, while rudimentary ovaries indicate Turner's syndrome.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Mendel's monohybrid cross (TT × tt) are correct?\n(i) The F1 plants have genotype Tt but phenotype tall.\n(ii) The F2 genotypic ratio is 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt.\n(iii) Dwarf F2 plants, when selfed, produce only dwarf plants in F3 and F4.\n(iv) TT and Tt plants of F2 can be told apart by their appearance.\n(v) Segregation of alleles into gametes is random, giving a 50% chance of a gamete carrying either allele.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iii) and (v) are correct. Statement (iv) is wrong because dominance makes TT and Tt look identical, which is why a test cross is needed to distinguish them.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In birds such as the chicken, the sex of the offspring is decided by:","image":"","options":["The sperm, because males are ZW and produce two kinds of gametes","The sperm, because males produce Z-bearing and W-bearing gametes","The number of chromosome sets received, as in the honey bee","The egg, because females are ZW and produce two kinds of gametes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Birds show female heterogamety. Females (ZW) produce Z-bearing and W-bearing eggs, while males (ZZ) produce only Z sperms, so the egg decides the sex of the chick. This is the reverse of humans, where the sperm decides.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"To find out whether a tall F2 pea plant is TT or Tt, Mendel crossed it with:","image":"","options":["Another tall F2 plant","A dwarf (tt) plant","A true-breeding tall (TT) plant","The tall F1 (Tt) plant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a test cross, a plant showing the dominant phenotype is crossed with the recessive parent (tt). This lets the progeny reveal the unknown genotype. Crossing with TT would give all tall progeny and reveal nothing.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In snapdragon, flower colour shows incomplete dominance (RR red, Rr pink, rr white). Assume a second gene for plant height assorts independently, with T (tall) completely dominant over t (dwarf). A plant of genotype RrTt is self-pollinated. How many phenotypic classes appear in the progeny, and what fraction is pink and tall?","image":"","options":["6 classes; $\\frac{3}{8}$","4 classes; $\\frac{9}{16}$","6 classes; $\\frac{3}{16}$","9 classes; $\\frac{1}{4}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Flower colour gives 3 phenotypes (1:2:1) and height gives 2 phenotypes (3:1), so there are $3 \\times 2 = 6$ classes. The fraction that is pink and tall is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{3}{8}$. The trap is applying the 9:3:3:1 pattern even though one gene is incompletely dominant.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cross in pea; R = round, Y = yellow, both dominant) with List-II (expected phenotypic outcome).\nList-I\nA. RrYy × RrYy\nB. RrYy × rryy\nC. RRYy × RRYy\nD. RrYy × RRYY\nList-II\nI. 1 : 1 : 1 : 1\nII. 3 : 1\nIII. 9 : 3 : 3 : 1\nIV. All round, yellow\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A dihybrid selfed gives 9:3:3:1, and a dihybrid test cross gives 1:1:1:1. In RRYy × RRYy only the colour gene segregates, giving 3:1. In D, the RRYY parent contributes both dominant alleles, so every offspring is round and yellow.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In haemophilia, a simple cut can cause non-stop bleeding. What is the basic defect?","image":"","options":["A single protein of the cascade of proteins involved in blood clotting is affected","Red blood cells become sickle-shaped and block capillaries","Too few globin chains are synthesised","Platelets are completely absent from blood"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Haemophilia is a sex-linked recessive disease in which one protein of the clotting cascade is affected, so blood fails to clot. Sickling and reduced globin synthesis describe sickle-cell anaemia and thalassemia respectively.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"The F2 genotypic ratio of a monohybrid cross can be obtained by expanding the binomial $(\\frac{1}{2}T + \\frac{1}{2}t)^2$. What is the coefficient of the Tt term in this expansion?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{3}{4}$","$1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The expansion gives $\\frac{1}{4}TT + \\frac{1}{2}Tt + \\frac{1}{4}tt$. The Tt term is $2 \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2}$ because a T pollen can meet a t egg and a t pollen can meet a T egg. Writing 1/4 ignores one of these two routes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about sex determination in the honey bee are correct?\n(i) A drone develops from an unfertilised egg by parthenogenesis.\n(ii) Drones produce sperm by meiosis.\n(iii) A worker bee has 32 chromosomes.\n(iv) A drone has a grandfather but no father.\n(v) A drone can have sons.\n(vi) Both the queen and the workers develop from fertilised eggs.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (iii), (iv) and (vi) are correct, which makes four. Drones are haploid (16 chromosomes), so they produce sperm by mitosis, not meiosis, which rules out (ii). Drones also cannot have sons, because sons arise from unfertilised eggs, which rules out (v).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Co-dominance\nB. Incomplete dominance\nC. Multiple alleles\nD. Complete dominance\nList-II\nI. Pink-flowered Rr snapdragon\nII. Tall Tt pea plant\nIII. $I^AI^B$ person with both A and B sugars on RBCs\nIV. Three alleles of gene I in the human population\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The $I^AI^B$ person expresses both alleles, which is co-dominance. The pink Rr snapdragon is intermediate between red and white, which is incomplete dominance. Gene I having three alleles is a case of multiple alleles. The Tt pea plant looks exactly like the TT parent, which is complete dominance.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Polygenic trait\n(b) Pleiotropic gene\n(c) AaBbCc (three-gene skin colour model)\n(d) Phenylalanine hydroxylase\nList-II\n(i) Intermediate skin colour\n(ii) Enzyme affected in phenylketonuria\n(iii) Human height\n(iv) Single gene with multiple phenotypic expressions","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Human height is polygenic, and a pleiotropic gene is a single gene with several phenotypic effects. AaBbCc carries three dominant and three recessive alleles, so it has an intermediate colour. Phenylalanine hydroxylase is the enzyme affected in phenylketonuria.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a Bb pea seed, allele B behaves as dominant when seed shape is considered but shows incomplete dominance when starch grain size is considered.\nStatement II: Whether an allele is dominant is a fixed, autonomous property of the allele and does not depend on which phenotype is examined.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Bb seeds are round like BB seeds, but their starch grains are of intermediate size, so Statement I is correct. This very example shows that dominance is not autonomous. It depends on the gene product and on the phenotype chosen, so Statement II is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\nI. Pea seeds of genotype Bb are round at maturity.\nII. Pea seeds of genotype Bb contain starch grains of intermediate size compared with BB and bb seeds.\nIII. A modified allele that still produces a normal or less efficient enzyme always gives a recessive phenotype that is distinct from that of the unmodified allele.\nIV. In the snapdragon flower-colour cross, the F2 genotypic ratio differs from the genotypic ratio of a typical Mendelian monohybrid cross.\nV. A recessive trait is commonly seen because the modified allele produces a non-functional enzyme or no enzyme at all.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements I, II and V are correct. Bb seeds are round but have intermediate-sized starch grains, so dominance depends on which phenotype is examined. III is wrong because an allele that still makes a normal or less efficient enzyme is equivalent to the unmodified allele and gives the same phenotype. IV is wrong because snapdragon shows the usual 1:2:1 genotypic ratio; only its phenotypic ratio changes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A man with blood group AB marries a woman with blood group A. The woman's father had blood group O. Which of the following correctly describes the blood groups expected among their children?","image":"","options":["1/4 of the children are expected to have blood group O","1/2 of the children are expected to have blood group AB","1/4 of the children are expected to have blood group B","All the children will have blood group A or AB"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The woman must be $I^Ai$, since she received $i$ from her O-group ($ii$) father. Crossing $I^AI^B \\times I^Ai$ gives $I^AI^A$, $I^Ai$, $I^AI^B$ and $I^Bi$ in equal proportions, so the children are 1/2 A, 1/4 AB and 1/4 B. No O child is possible because the father carries no $i$ allele.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In the F2 generation of a monohybrid cross between true-breeding tall (TT) and dwarf (tt) pea plants, what fraction of the TALL plants would breed true on self-pollination?","image":"","options":["1/4","1/3","1/2","2/3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Among F2 plants, 1/4 are TT and 1/2 are Tt, so TT makes up (1/4)/(3/4) = 1/3 of the tall plants. Only TT plants breed true. The trap is answering 1/4, which is the fraction of TT among all F2 plants.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements about honey bees:\nStatement I: A drone develops from an unfertilised egg by parthenogenesis and has 16 chromosomes.\nStatement II: A drone produces sperms by meiosis, which halves its chromosome number.\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Drones are haploid (16 chromosomes) because they develop from unfertilised eggs. Being haploid, they produce sperms by mitosis, not meiosis.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Thalassemia is a quantitative problem of synthesising too few globin molecules, whereas sickle-cell anaemia is a qualitative problem of synthesising an incorrectly functioning globin.\nStatement II: $\\alpha$ Thalassemia is controlled by a single gene HBB on chromosome 11 of each parent.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I correctly states how the two disorders differ. Statement II is wrong: $\\alpha$ Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes, HBA1 and HBA2, on chromosome 16. HBB on chromosome 11 controls $\\beta$ Thalassemia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A woman with normal vision, whose father was red-green colour blind, marries a colour-blind man.\nStatement I: About 50% of their sons are expected to be colour blind.\nStatement II: About 50% of their daughters are expected to be colour blind.\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The woman must be a carrier ($X^cX$), since she received $X^c$ from her father. Half her sons receive $X^c$ and are colour blind. Every daughter receives $X^c$ from the father, so half the daughters are $X^cX^c$ (affected) and half are carriers. The common trap is assuming daughters are never affected.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the postulates of Mendel's Law of Dominance?","image":"","options":["Characters are controlled by discrete units called factors","Factors occur in pairs","In a dissimilar pair of factors, one member dominates the other","The two factors of a pair separate so that each gamete receives only one"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Separation of the paired factors into gametes is the Law of Segregation. The other three statements are the postulates of the Law of Dominance.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Individuals with Klinefelter's syndrome show overall masculine development but are sterile.\nStatement II: Individuals with Turner's syndrome have 45 chromosomes and are phenotypically male.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Klinefelter's (47, XXY) individuals are masculine overall, with some feminine development, and are sterile, so Statement I is correct. Turner's individuals (45, XO) are sterile females, not males, so Statement II is incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"An individual whose cells lack one chromosome of any one homologous pair is said to show:","image":"","options":["Monosomy","Trisomy","Polyploidy","Triploidy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Losing one chromosome of a pair is monosomy (2n − 1), and having an extra copy is trisomy (2n + 1). Polyploidy and triploidy involve whole extra sets of chromosomes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A pedigree shows the following features:\n- The trait appears in every generation.\n- An affected father and an unaffected mother have both affected sons and affected daughters.\n- Unaffected children of affected parents have only unaffected offspring.\nThe trait is most likely:","image":"","options":["Autosomal recessive, like sickle-cell anaemia","X-linked recessive, like haemophilia","Autosomal dominant, like myotonic dystrophy","Y-linked, passed only from father to son"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Appearance in every generation, with unaffected individuals never transmitting the trait, points to a dominant allele. Father-to-son transmission rules out X-linkage, and affected daughters rule out Y-linkage. The pattern is therefore autosomal dominant.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the molecular basis of sickle-cell anaemia is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It results from a single base substitution at the sixth codon of the $\\beta$-globin gene, changing GAG to GUG","The mutant haemoglobin polymerises under low oxygen tension","Polymerisation changes the RBC from a biconcave disc to an elongated sickle-like shape","It is a frame-shift mutation that alters every amino acid after position 6 of the $\\beta$ chain"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Sickle-cell anaemia is a point mutation, a single base substitution, that changes only one amino acid (Glu to Val). Frame-shift mutations arise from insertions or deletions of base pairs, not substitutions.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\nA. Colour blindness\nB. Phenylketonuria\nC. $\\beta$ Thalassemia\nD. Sickle-cell anaemia\nList-II:\nI. Accumulation of phenylpyruvic acid\nII. Defect in red or green cone of the eye\nIII. Reduced synthesis of $\\beta$-globin chains\nIV. Valine in place of glutamic acid in $\\beta$-globin\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Colour blindness comes from a defective red or green cone, and PKU causes phenylpyruvic acid to accumulate. $\\beta$ thalassemia lowers the amount of $\\beta$-globin made, while sickle-cell anaemia changes its sequence (Glu to Val). Do not confuse a reduced quantity of globin with an altered structure.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A couple already has three daughters. What is the probability that their next child will be a son, and which gamete decides the child's sex?","image":"","options":["25 per cent; the ovum","50 per cent; the sperm","75 per cent; the sperm","50 per cent; the ovum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each pregnancy is an independent event. Half the sperm carry X and half carry Y, while every ovum carries X. So the chance of a son is always 50 per cent, and it is the sperm that decides sex. Earlier births do not change this.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A gene that shows multiple phenotypic expressions is called a:","image":"","options":["Polygenic gene","Co-dominant gene","Pleiotropic gene","Multiple allele"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A pleiotropic gene is a single gene that produces more than one phenotypic effect. Polygenic inheritance is the reverse situation, where many genes together control one trait.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about human ABO blood groups:\n(i) The gene I has three alleles.\n(ii) Six different genotypes are possible.\n(iii) Four different phenotypes are possible.\n(iv) Allele i produces a third distinct type of sugar on the RBC surface.\n(v) $I^A$ and $I^B$ are co-dominant with each other.\n(vi) Multiple alleles can be detected by studying a single individual.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iii) and (v) are correct. Allele i produces no sugar at all, and an individual carries only two alleles, so multiple alleles can be detected only through population studies.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In the karyotype of a male child with Down's syndrome, the numbers of autosomes and sex chromosomes are, respectively:","image":"","options":["44 and 3","44 and 2","45 and 2","46 and 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A normal male has 44 autosomes plus XY. Down's syndrome adds an extra chromosome 21, which is an autosome, giving 45 autosomes and 2 sex chromosomes (total 47). The trap '44 and 3' describes Klinefelter's syndrome, where the extra chromosome is an X.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Red-green colour blindness occurs in about 8 per cent of males but only about 0.4 per cent of females. Which of the following best explains this difference?","image":"","options":["The defective genes lie on the X chromosome, and males have only one X chromosome","The defective genes lie on the Y chromosome, which only males carry","The defective allele is autosomal dominant and is expressed more strongly in males","Females have extra cone cells in the eye that compensate for the defect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Colour blindness is X-linked recessive. A male with a single defective X is affected. A female needs two defective X chromosomes, because one normal dominant allele masks the effect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In the honey bee, a drone (male) develops from:","image":"","options":["a fertilised egg that receives an extra X-chromosome","a fertilised egg fed on a special diet","an unfertilised egg, by parthenogenesis","a fertilised egg that loses one set of chromosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Drones develop from unfertilised eggs by parthenogenesis, so they are haploid. Fertilised eggs develop into females, either queens or workers.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"$\\beta$ Thalassemia is controlled by a single gene, HBB. On which human chromosome is HBB located?","image":"","options":["Chromosome 16","Chromosome 21","Chromosome 11","X chromosome"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"HBB lies on chromosome 11 of each parent. Chromosome 16 is the common trap because it carries HBA1 and HBA2, the genes for $\\alpha$ thalassemia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In pea, tall (T) is dominant over dwarf (t) and round seed (R) is dominant over wrinkled (r). The two genes assort independently. A plant of genotype TtRr is crossed with a plant of genotype ttRr. What proportion of the offspring will be dwarf with wrinkled seeds?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{16}$","$\\frac{3}{8}$","$\\frac{1}{8}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Treat each gene separately. Tt × tt gives $\\frac{1}{2}$ dwarf, and Rr × Rr gives $\\frac{1}{4}$ wrinkled, so the combined proportion is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{8}$. $\\frac{1}{16}$ would apply only if both parents were double heterozygotes.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In birds, the egg (female gamete) determines the sex of the chick.\nStatement II: Female birds carry two similar sex chromosomes, ZZ.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Birds show female heterogamety. Females are ZW and produce two kinds of eggs, so the egg decides the sex of the chick. Males, not females, are ZZ, which makes Statement II wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Down's syndrome\nB. Klinefelter's syndrome\nC. Turner's syndrome\nD. Polyploidy\nList-II\nI. Rudimentary ovaries and lack of secondary sexual characters\nII. Overall masculine development along with gynaecomastia\nIII. Furrowed tongue, partially open mouth and characteristic palm crease\nIV. Increase in a whole set of chromosomes, often seen in plants\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Down's syndrome (trisomy 21) shows a furrowed tongue, partially open mouth and a characteristic palm crease. Klinefelter's (47, XXY) males show gynaecomastia, Turner's (45, XO) females have rudimentary ovaries, and polyploidy adds whole chromosome sets. A common trap is swapping the Klinefelter's and Turner's features.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"A gene codes for an enzyme that converts substrate S. A modified allele of this gene could produce a normal/less efficient enzyme, a non-functional enzyme, or no enzyme. In which of these cases is the modified allele equivalent to the unmodified allele and produces the same phenotype?","image":"","options":["When it produces a non-functional enzyme","When it produces no enzyme at all","When it produces a normal or less efficient enzyme","In all three cases equally"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"If the modified allele still makes a normal or less efficient enzyme, substrate S is still transformed. The allele is therefore equivalent to the original. The phenotype may be affected only when the enzyme is non-functional or absent.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Thalassemia is described as a quantitative problem, whereas sickle-cell anaemia is a qualitative problem of globin synthesis.\nReason (R): Thalassemia is an X-linked recessive disorder, whereas sickle-cell anaemia is an autosomal recessive disorder.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: thalassemia means too few globin molecules are made, while sickle-cell anaemia means an incorrectly functioning globin is made. R is false because both disorders are autosome-linked recessive.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Dominance is not an autonomous feature of a gene; it depends on which phenotype is examined.\nReason (R): In pea, Bb seeds are round like BB seeds, but their starch grains are of intermediate size.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For seed shape, B behaves as completely dominant. For starch grain size, the same alleles show incomplete dominance. This one gene therefore illustrates that dominance depends on the phenotype chosen.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Pedigree analysis is used to study the inheritance of traits in human beings.\nReason (R): Controlled crosses of the kind performed on pea plants are not possible in humans.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Humans cannot be subjected to controlled crosses. Studying the family history of a trait over several generations is therefore the alternative used. R directly explains why A is practised.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following characters of the garden pea were among the seven characters studied by Mendel?\nStem height, Pod shape, Leaf shape, Flower position, Seed colour, Fruit size, Flower colour","image":"","options":["4","6","3","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Five of the listed characters appear in Table 4.1: stem height, pod shape, flower position, seed colour and flower colour. Leaf shape and fruit size were not among Mendel's seven characters.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In the three-gene additive model of human skin colour (A, B, C), two individuals of genotype AaBbCc marry. What fraction of their children is expected to have the same skin colour as the parents?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{64}$","$\\frac{15}{64}$","$\\frac{5}{16}$","$\\frac{3}{32}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each parent passes on 3 alleles, so a child receives 6 alleles, each with a $\\frac{1}{2}$ chance of being dominant. Having exactly 3 dominant alleles (the parental shade) has probability $\\frac{^6C_3}{2^6} = \\frac{20}{64} = \\frac{5}{16}$. The value $\\frac{15}{64}$ is the trap: it corresponds to 2 or 4 dominant alleles.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Morgan chose the fruit fly Drosophila melanogaster to test the chromosomal theory of inheritance. Which of the following was NOT a reason for this choice?","image":"","options":["It can be reared in the laboratory on a simple synthetic medium","It completes its life cycle in about two weeks","Male and female flies look alike and cannot be easily told apart","A single mating produces a large number of progeny flies"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Drosophila shows clear sexual differentiation, so males and females are easy to distinguish. That made it more useful for genetic crosses. The other three options are advantages NCERT lists.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The existence of multiple alleles for the ABO blood group gene can be recognised only when population studies are made.\nReason (R): A diploid individual can possess at most two of the alleles of a gene.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Any one person carries only two of the three I alleles, so all three can be observed only by examining many individuals in a population. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Mutation alters the DNA sequence and can change both genotype and phenotype.\n(ii) Besides recombination, mutation is another phenomenon that leads to variation in DNA.\n(iii) Chromosomal aberrations are commonly observed in cancer cells.\n(iv) Insertion or deletion of base pairs in DNA causes point mutations.\n(v) UV radiation can cause mutations in organisms.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iii) and (v) are correct. Statement (iv) is wrong because insertions or deletions of base pairs cause frame-shift mutations, whereas a point mutation is a change in a single base pair.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I (Disorder)\nA. Down's syndrome\nB. Klinefelter's syndrome\nC. Turner's syndrome\nD. Sickle-cell anaemia\nList-II (Basis)\nI. 45, X0\nII. Glu replaced by Val at the sixth position of the $\\beta$-globin chain\nIII. Trisomy of chromosome 21\nIV. 47, XXY\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Down's syndrome is trisomy 21, Klinefelter's is 47, XXY and Turner's is 45, X0. Sickle-cell anaemia results from Glu being replaced by Val at position 6 of the $\\beta$-globin chain.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"Assuming all genes assort independently, how many genetically different types of gametes can an organism of genotype AaBbCCDdEe produce?","image":"","options":["16","8","32","10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The number of gamete types is $2^n$, where n is the number of heterozygous loci. Here A, B, D and E are heterozygous, so n = 4 and there are $2^4 = 16$ types. The CC locus is homozygous and adds no variety; counting it as heterozygous gives the trap answer 32.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-INHERITANCE-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","type":"mcq","question":"In the F2 generation of the dihybrid cross RRYY × rryy, match the item in List-I with its expected frequency in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Genotype RrYy\n(b) Genotype rrYy\n(c) Genotype RRyy\n(d) Phenotype round, yellow\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{1}{16}$\n(ii) $\\frac{2}{16}$\n(iii) $\\frac{4}{16}$\n(iv) $\\frac{9}{16}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each frequency is the product of the separate gene frequencies:\n- RrYy = $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{4}{16}$\n- rrYy = $\\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{2}{16}$\n- RRyy = $\\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{16}$\n- The round yellow phenotype pools four genotypes, totalling $\\frac{9}{16}$.\nThe trap is confusing the single genotype RrYy with the whole round yellow phenotype class.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 4 · Principles of Inheritance and Variation","difficulty":"medium"}]});
